### Also covered by

Mac Lane, *Categories for the Working Mathematician*, 2nd ed., §IV.3, book p. 92 (PDF p. 101), Exercise 1 — item `maclane:IV.3:ex1`. The exercise extends the §II.1 duality table with six further rows: functors (self-dual), fullness (self-dual), faithfulness (self-dual), natural transformations (direction reversed), adjunctions (the two functors exchanged and the transposes swapped), and unit-dualizes-to-counit.

Four of the six rows are already in force in-tree and can simply be cited when this issue is worked:

- `Functor/Opposite.v:31` — `Opposite_Functor`, with `Opposite_Functor_invol` at `:49`;
- `Natural/Transformation/Opposite.v:27` — `Opposite_Transform`, with the direction reversed;
- `Adjunction/Opposite.v:34` — `Opposite_Adjunction`, whose `to`/`from` are literally the transposes swapped, with `Opposite_Adjunction_invol` at `:60` proving the involution by `reflexivity`;
- `Adjunction/Natural/Transformation/Opposite.v:33` — `Opposite_Adjunction_Transform`, whose unit field is built from the counit and vice versa.

The genuine gap is the **fullness and faithfulness rows**: there are no lemmas of the shape `Full F → Full (F^op)` or `Faithful F → Faithful (F^op)`. `Theory/Equivalence/Limit.v:574`–`:575` obtains fullness and faithfulness of an opposite functor only through an equivalence detour, which does not cover an arbitrary full or faithful functor. Those two op-transport lemmas are the increment §IV.3 adds to this issue.
